WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.347
A job done in steps, one after
another, is an algorithm.

2
00:00:08.567 --> 00:00:12.097
You follow algorithms every
day. So does a computer.

3
00:00:12.597 --> 00:00:15.848
Robo wants toast. But Robo cannot guess.

4
00:00:16.068 --> 00:00:20.154
Robo only does what it is
told. You tell it every step.

5
00:00:20.374 --> 00:00:23.904
Take the bread. Put it in the
toaster. Push the lever down.

6
00:00:24.124 --> 00:00:27.189
Wait for it to pop up.
Then spread the butter.

7
00:00:27.409 --> 00:00:34.700
Toast! Those five steps are an
algorithm: a set of steps to do a job.

8
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.101
Algorithms are not only for
robots. You follow them every day.

9
00:00:39.321 --> 00:00:42.990
Getting dressed is one. Socks,
then shoes, then your coat.

10
00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:47.482
Brushing your teeth is one.
Paste on the brush. Brush. Rinse.

11
00:00:47.702 --> 00:00:52.253
A recipe is one. So is a
dance: step, step, clap, turn.

12
00:00:52.473 --> 00:00:57.117
An algorithm does not have to be
hard. Three steps can be enough.

13
00:00:57.617 --> 00:01:02.818
To follow an algorithm you do step
one, then step two, then step three.

14
00:01:03.038 --> 00:01:06.103
You never skip a step,
and you never jump ahead.

15
00:01:06.323 --> 00:01:09.992
Here are five steps for washing
your hands. Turn the tap on.

16
00:01:10.212 --> 00:01:14.020
Soap. Rub your hands together.
Rinse the bubbles away.

17
00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:17.770
Dry them. Five steps, in order,
and your hands are clean.

18
00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:22.217
You can write an algorithm too.
Think what has to happen first.

19
00:01:22.437 --> 00:01:27.128
Build a tower. The big brick goes
at the bottom, or the tower falls.

20
00:01:27.348 --> 00:01:31.713
Then the middle brick, then the
small one. The flag goes on last.

21
00:01:31.933 --> 00:01:36.252
Start with the flag, and it has nothing
under it. It lands on the floor.

22
00:01:36.472 --> 00:01:41.441
Now plant a seed. Get a pot, put
soil in it, plant the seed, water it.

23
00:01:41.661 --> 00:01:45.097
Water it first? Water
what? There is no seed yet.

24
00:01:45.597 --> 00:01:50.102
Lots of people follow algorithms
at work. A cook follows a recipe.

25
00:01:50.322 --> 00:01:55.430
A builder follows a plan. A nurse
follows steps to check you are well.

26
00:01:55.650 --> 00:02:00.387
A computer follows algorithms too.
Somebody always writes the steps.

27
00:02:00.607 --> 00:02:04.833
The steps are written in code, and
then the algorithm is a program.

28
00:02:05.333 --> 00:02:08.584
An algorithm gives the
same answer every time.

29
00:02:08.804 --> 00:02:12.426
Clean your teeth this way on
Monday. Do it again on Friday.

30
00:02:12.646 --> 00:02:17.523
Your teeth get clean both times.
That is why we write algorithms down.

31
00:02:17.743 --> 00:02:21.179
Anybody can pick it up, follow
it, and get the same result.

32
00:02:21.679 --> 00:02:26.463
An algorithm is a set of steps to
do a job, or to solve a problem.

33
00:02:26.683 --> 00:02:31.744
You follow one step at a time, and when
you write one the order has to work.

34
00:02:31.964 --> 00:02:36.005
Written in code, your algorithm
is a program a computer can run.
